# Refuse SuperSpeed for the Arducam B0495 on the Jetson Nano.
#
# WHY. These cameras are not reliable at 5000M on this board. Behind external
# hubs they produce flat green frames and flood dmesg with
# `uvcvideo: Non-zero status (-71) in video completion handler`; even hub-free
# and direct on the Nano, one logged 824 such errors in a single boot while
# serving ~19 fps against a requested 30. At 480M the same cameras run for hours
# with zero errors. See "The Nano's camera" in mast/README.md.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES. Deauthorises the SuperSpeed instance as it appears, which
# removes its /dev/video* node. The USB 2.0 wires are present in every USB3-A
# socket, so the camera is then free to come up at High Speed instead.
#
# WHAT IT IS PROVEN TO DO: remove the stale SuperSpeed twin that a wedged camera
# leaves behind. That ghost is not cosmetic — it consumes a port in
# nano-camera.sh and shifts every camera after it, which silently re-maps the
# UI tiles.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN: that a camera whose ONLY enumeration is SuperSpeed will
# re-train at High Speed after being deauthorised, rather than simply going
# away. If a camera disappears entirely after installing this, that is the case
# you have hit — the fix is a USB 2.0 cable, and this rule is one file to
# delete. Nothing here is destructive: `echo 1 > .../authorized` undoes it live.
#
# Install:  sudo cp 99-arducam-no-superspeed.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#           sudo udevadm control --reload
# Remove:   sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/99-arducam-no-superspeed.rules
#
# 04b4:4950 is the Cypress FX3 bridge the B0495 presents. ATTR{speed}=="5000"
# is what confines this to SuperSpeed — the 480M instance is untouched.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="04b4", ATTR{idProduct}=="4950", ATTR{speed}=="5000", ATTR{authorized}="0"
